<div class="note"><strong>Source note.</strong> The novel's full text was not part of the material supplied, only the two worksheet answer sheets. This guide therefore relies on those sheets plus my general knowledge of the plot. It contains <strong>no direct quotations</strong> from the book (only the two short phrases that appear in your worksheet questions), and it does not give page numbers or invented chapter titles. Check details against your own edition. Chapter numbers are mentioned only where your worksheets give them (Chapter 68; Chapters 25–35).</div>

<section id="sum"><h2>1. Summary of the novel</h2>
<table><tr><th>Title / author</th><td><em>We Were Liars</em>, E. Lockhart</td></tr>
<tr><th>Genre</th><td>Young adult psychological mystery with a tragic twist</td></tr>
<tr><th>Setting</th><td>Beechwood Island, a private island off the coast of Massachusetts owned by the wealthy Sinclair family. Each family branch has its own house; the biggest is Clairmont, where the grandparents live.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Narrator</th><td>Cadence Sinclair Eastman ("Cady"), first person, unreliable</td></tr>
<tr><th>Central conflict</th><td>Cadence cannot remember what happened in "summer fifteen" and tries to recover the truth, while the family hides its problems behind a perfect image.</td></tr></table>

<h3>The Sinclair family</h3>
<p>The Sinclairs are rich, white, privileged and proud of looking perfect. Granddad controls the family through money and inheritance. His three daughters (Penny, Carrie and Bess) compete for his approval and for the houses. Beneath the beautiful surface are greed, secrets, control and, as Gat sees, racism.</p>

<h3>Main events in order</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Earlier summers:</strong> Cadence, her cousins Johnny and Mirren, and Gat (Ed's nephew) become the inseparable "Liars". Cadence and Gat fall in love.</li>
<li><strong>Summer fifteen:</strong> something terrible happens. Cadence is found injured on the beach. She suffers memory loss and severe migraines. She is told it was a swimming accident.</li>
<li><strong>Two years of silence:</strong> Gat does not contact her. The family tells her little.</li>
<li><strong>Summer seventeen:</strong> Cadence returns. Clairmont has been destroyed and replaced by a new house. She tries to recover her memory with the Liars, while she notices the adults' secrets and arguments (Chapters 25–35 in your worksheet).</li>
<li><strong>The reveal:</strong> the Liars spread gasoline and burned Clairmont (the idea comes in Chapter 68). The fire spread too fast. Gat, Johnny and Mirren died; Cadence escaped badly burned.</li>
<li><strong>Ending:</strong> Cadence accepts the truth, that the other three are dead and she took part in the fire. Her mother Penny still loves her.</li></ol>

<h3>Meaning of the title</h3>
<p>The Liars are four teenagers who keep a "lie" at the centre of their friendship. The title also points to the wider lying: the family lies about its happiness, the adults lie to Cadence about the past, and Cadence's own mind lies to protect her from trauma. <em>We</em> includes the narrator and shows she is not an innocent observer.</p></section>

<section id="truth"><h2>2. Belief versus truth</h2>
<div class="tw"><table>
<tr><th>Topic</th><th>What Cadence believes</th><th>What is really true</th></tr>
<tr><td>Summer fifteen</td><td>A swimming accident caused her injury and memory loss.</td><td>The "accident" was the fire at Clairmont; the swimming story is a false memory covering the truth.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clairmont</td><td>It was simply replaced.</td><td>The Liars burned it down to stop the family's fighting over money and possessions.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gat, Johnny, Mirren</td><td>They are alive and are with her on the island.</td><td>They died in the fire (worksheet: the fire spread too quickly and trapped them inside).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Her injuries</td><td>Headaches and scars from an accident.</td><td>Burns to her hands and feet from trying to save them.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Her role</td><td>A victim who lost her memory.</td><td>A participant in the plan: "we" burned Clairmont.</td></tr>
<tr><td>The dogs</td><td>Not an issue.</td><td>Fatima and Prince Philip died too, locked in an upstairs room (worksheet).</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Cadence's mind protects her from guilt and grief, so the reader sees a world that feels warm and nostalgic but is slightly "off". The truth is released gradually.</p></section>

<section id="chars"><h2>3. Characters</h2>
<div class="tw"><table>
<tr><th>Character</th><th>Traits and role</th><th>Development / significance</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Cadence (Cady)</strong></td><td>Curious, determined, confused, painful migraines but tries to stay strong. Wants the truth.</td><td>Moves from idealising the family to seeing its greed and lies. Her reliability is questioned throughout.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Gat</strong></td><td>Intelligent, thoughtful, caring. Makes Cadence think about money, privilege and unfairness. Outsider because of his background; Ed's nephew.</td><td>Loves Cadence, but is not always clear about the past (he asks if they can forget it and start over). Voice of social criticism. Dies in the fire.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Johnny</strong></td><td>Funny, energetic, athletic, sometimes careless; uses jokes instead of serious talk.</td><td>Humour as avoidance; his carelessness links to the tragedy. Dies in the fire.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Mirren</strong></td><td>Kind, cheerful, caring; sometimes avoids difficult questions and hides information.</td><td>Shows the Liars' evasiveness when Cadence asks about summer fifteen. Dies in the fire.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Penny, Carrie, Bess</strong></td><td>The Sinclair sisters: care about the family but are strongly influenced by money and Granddad's approval; hide problems and compete over houses and inheritance.</td><td>Represent the family's greed and dishonesty. Penny, Cadence's mother, still loves and protects her.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Granddad (Harris) and Grandma</strong></td><td>Controlling, wealthy. Granddad uses inheritance as a weapon (he threatens Carrie's inheritance if she marries Ed).</td><td>Their behaviour towards Ed and Gat shows racism (see Q1).</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Ed</strong></td><td>Gat's uncle, Carrie's partner.</td><td>Granddad calls him "sir" and keeps him at a distance: exposes prejudice.</td></tr></table></div></section>

<section id="themes"><h2>4. Themes</h2>
<h3>Wealth, privilege and materialism</h3>
<p>The Sinclairs' money controls relationships. Houses and inheritance are used as rewards and punishments. Gat notices unfairness that the family ignores. <em>Model line:</em> "Lockhart presents wealth not as a source of happiness but as a force that corrupts family bonds."</p>
<h3>Family expectations and the pressure to appear perfect</h3>
<p>The family looks like a beautiful, special family, but it hides its problems. Cadence's fairy-tale style and the island's beauty contrast with arguments about money.</p>
<h3>Truth, lies and unreliable narration</h3>
<p>Lying is everywhere: the family, the Liars and Cadence's memory. The ending reveals that the "swimming accident" was a false memory.</p>
<h3>Memory and trauma</h3>
<p>Cadence's amnesia and migraines are the effect of trauma. She has only fragments (gasoline, the Liars laughing) she cannot connect.</p>
<h3>Guilt, responsibility and consequences</h3>
<p>The Liars want to do good but the plan is dangerous and badly coordinated. Their idealism has tragic consequences.</p>
<h3>Racism and prejudice</h3>
<p>Granddad's treatment of Ed and Gat, and Grandma's discomfort, reveal that the family does not fully accept them.</p>
<h3>Love and loyalty</h3>
<p>Cadence's love for Gat, her friends, and Penny's unconditional love "in spite of" what Cadence may have done.</p></section>

<section id="tech"><h2>5. Literary techniques</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Unreliable narrator:</strong> Cadence's gaps and false memory mean the reader must question what she says.</li>
<li><strong>Foreshadowing and suspense:</strong> fragments such as gasoline, the Liars laughing, and their strange behaviour when asked questions hint at the truth.</li>
<li><strong>Contrast of appearance and reality:</strong> the island looks perfect; the family is greedy and dishonest.</li>
<li><strong>Setting as symbol:</strong> Clairmont stands for greed, racism, control and love of money (Q4). Its destruction is meant to purify the family.</li>
<li><strong>Fire and destruction:</strong> an attempt to cleanse that results in tragedy and irony: a plan to improve the family destroys what they love.</li>
<li><strong>Irony:</strong> the Liars try to remove "evil" but cause death.</li>
<li><strong>Gradual release of information:</strong> the reader pieces the truth together alongside Cadence.</li></ul></section>

<section id="qs"><h2>6. Your 13 questions with model answers</h2>
<p>Open each question. "Short version" is for memorising. Mistakes to avoid are listed for each.</p>

<h3>Worksheet A: Chapters 25–35</h3>

<details><summary>A1. What does Cadence discover about events on the island, and how does this change her understanding of her memories?</summary><div>
<p><strong>Asks:</strong> what has she learned about the island, and does it change how she trusts her memory?</p>
<p><strong>Model answer:</strong> Cadence discovers that the old Clairmont house has been destroyed and replaced by a new one, so important events must have happened in summer fifteen that she cannot remember. Because nobody explains these events clearly, she begins to doubt that the official story of an accident is complete. Her memory, once something she trusted, becomes a puzzle: she has only fragments of images, such as gasoline and the Liars laughing, which do not fit a simple accident. This marks the shift from passively accepting the family's version to actively searching for the truth.</p>
<p class="short"><strong>Short:</strong> Clairmont is gone → big events happened that she can't recall → she doubts her memory of the "accident".</p>
<p><strong>Avoid:</strong> only saying "she lost her memory" without linking it to the destroyed house and her doubt.</p></div></details>

<details><summary>A2. Why are Gat, Mirren, and Johnny important to Cadence during this part of the story?</summary><div>
<p><strong>Model answer:</strong> They are Cadence's closest friends and, with her, make up the Liars. They make her feel happy and normal again after two difficult years of pain and isolation. She also hopes that they can help her remember summer fifteen. Ironically, they are also the ones who act strangely when she asks questions, so they are both her comfort and a source of mystery. (Later we learn why: they are dead, and she is not seeing events as they really are.)</p>
<p class="short"><strong>Short:</strong> closest friends → make her feel normal → she hopes they will help her remember.</p>
<p><strong>Avoid:</strong> describing them as only "friends" without the memory link.</p></div></details>

<details><summary>A3. What does Cadence begin to realize about her family's behavior?</summary><div>
<p><strong>Model answer:</strong> Cadence begins to realise that the Sinclair family hides its problems. The adults argue about money, houses and inheritance, while pretending to be perfect. Seen more clearly, they are controlling and dishonest with each other, using Granddad's approval and wealth as a form of power. This changes her view of the family from a fairy tale to a more critical, realistic picture.</p>
<p class="short"><strong>Short:</strong> they hide problems, fight about money, pretend to be perfect, and are controlling and dishonest.</p></div></details>

<details><summary>A4. How does the relationship between Cadence and Gat change in Chapters 25-35?</summary><div>
<p><strong>Model answer:</strong> Cadence still loves Gat, but she is angry because he did not contact her for two years. They become close again, but they also argue. Gat asks whether they can forget the past and start over, which shows his wish for a fresh beginning but also hints that he is avoiding something. Their relationship is therefore both romantic and tense.</p>
<p class="short"><strong>Short:</strong> still love, anger over two years of silence, closeness and arguments, Gat wants to start over.</p>
<p><strong>Avoid:</strong> saying only "they fall in love again".</p></div></details>

<details><summary>A5. What clues suggest that something is wrong with Cadence's memories?</summary><div>
<p><strong>Model answer:</strong> Several clues suggest her memory is unreliable. She cannot recall important moments from summer fifteen. The Liars act strangely when she asks questions. She has small, disconnected images such as gasoline and the Liars laughing that she cannot link together. Together they foreshadow the truth: that the "accident" was a fire and that her memory is hiding trauma.</p>
<p class="short"><strong>Short:</strong> blank summer fifteen, strange Liars, fragments (gasoline, laughing).</p></div></details>

<details><summary>A6. How do Cadence's feelings about the Sinclair family change?</summary><div>
<p><strong>Model answer:</strong> At first, Cadence sees the Sinclairs as a beautiful and special family. Later, she notices their secrets, greed, arguments and lies. She still loves them, but she no longer believes that the family is perfect. This mirrors the novel's wider movement from illusion to reality.</p>
<p class="short"><strong>Short:</strong> perfect → secrets and greed → still love, but no illusion.</p></div></details>

<h3>Worksheet B: Simple answers</h3>

<details><summary>B1. What signs show that Granddad and Grandma might be racist?</summary><div>
<p><strong>Model answer:</strong> Granddad does not call Gat by his name, calls Ed "sir" and keeps him at a distance. He also threatens to take away Carrie's inheritance if she marries Ed. Grandma looks uncomfortable when she meets Ed. Together these details suggest that they do not fully accept Ed and Gat because of their skin colour. Note the word <em>might</em>: the evidence is behaviour, not open statements.</p>
<p class="short"><strong>Short:</strong> no name for Gat, "sir" for Ed, inheritance threat, Grandma uncomfortable.</p></div></details>

<details><summary>B2. Why does Gat think the island and the family are not perfect?</summary><div>
<p><strong>Model answer:</strong> The island looks beautiful, but the family has many problems. They are greedy and fight about money, houses and inheritance. Granddad controls the family with his money. Gat also sees racism in the family. As an outsider he sees that the Sinclairs look perfect from outside but are not happy or honest.</p>
<p class="short"><strong>Short:</strong> greed, control by money, racism, unhappy and dishonest behind the image.</p></div></details>

<details><summary>B3. Why is Chapter 68 important?</summary><div>
<p><strong>Model answer:</strong> Chapter 68 gives the Liars the idea to burn Clairmont. The family is fighting about money and possessions, and the Liars believe that destroying the house will stop the fighting and make the family change. However, the plan is very dangerous, and this chapter is therefore the turning point that leads to the tragedy.</p>
<p class="short"><strong>Short:</strong> origin of the plan: burn Clairmont to stop the fighting; dangerous.</p></div></details>

<details><summary>B4. What does Cady mean by "We changed an evil we saw in the world"?</summary><div>
<p><strong>Model answer:</strong> "We" means Cady, Gat, Johnny and Mirren. They burned Clairmont because it represented greed, racism, control and the family's love of money. They believed that destroying it would make the family better. The sentence shows their idealism, but also the irony that they did harm in trying to do good.</p>
<p class="short"><strong>Short:</strong> the Liars burned Clairmont, a symbol of greed, racism and control, hoping to improve the family.</p></div></details>

<details><summary>B5. Who died in the fire, and why?</summary><div>
<p><strong>Model answer:</strong> The dogs, Fatima and Prince Philip, died because they were locked in an upstairs room and could not escape. Gat, Johnny and Mirren also died because the fire spread too quickly and trapped them inside the house.</p>
<p class="short"><strong>Short:</strong> two dogs + Gat, Johnny, Mirren; trapped as the fire spread too fast.</p><p><strong>Avoid:</strong> forgetting the dogs or saying Cady died.</p></div></details>

<details><summary>B6. What really happened on the night of the accident?</summary><div>
<p><strong>Model answer:</strong> The four Liars spread gasoline around Clairmont and set it on fire. They did not agree on the exact time to start, so the fire spread too fast. Cady escaped, but Gat, Johnny and Mirren were trapped. Cady tried to save them and burned her hands and feet. Her "swimming accident" was a false memory hiding the truth.</p>
<p class="short"><strong>Short:</strong> Liars burned Clairmont → timing mistake → three trapped → Cady burned trying to save them → "swimming accident" = false memory.</p></div></details>

<details><summary>B7. What does Cady mean when she thinks her mother loves her "in spite of" what she may have done?</summary><div>
<p><strong>Model answer:</strong> Cady understands that her mother probably suspects she helped start the fire. Even so, Penny still loves, protects and cares for Cady. Her love is stronger than her anger, sadness and suspicion. It shows unconditional love as a counterpoint to the family's conditional, money-based affection.</p>
<p class="short"><strong>Short:</strong> Penny suspects but loves anyway; unconditional love.</p></div></details>
</section>

<section id="tl"><h2>7. Timeline and key facts</h2>
<div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Event</th></tr>
<tr><td>Before summer fifteen</td><td>Cadence, Gat, Johnny and Mirren form the Liars; family tensions over money and Ed/Gat grow</td></tr>
<tr><td>Summer fifteen</td><td>Plan to burn Clairmont (Ch. 68 gives the idea), fire, three Liars and two dogs die, Cadence burned</td></tr>
<tr><td>Afterwards</td><td>Memory loss, migraines, "swimming accident" story, Gat silent two years</td></tr>
<tr><td>Summer seventeen</td><td>Returns, new house, clues, reunion, truth recovered</td></tr></table></div>
<h3>Facts to know before the exam</h3>
<ol>
<li>Narrator: Cadence Sinclair Eastman, unreliable, first person.</li>
<li>Four Liars: Cadence, Gat, Johnny, Mirren.</li>
<li>Setting: private Sinclair island with several family houses; Clairmont is the grandparents' house.</li>
<li>Penny, Carrie and Bess compete over houses and inheritance.</li>
<li>Granddad controls the family with money; threatens Carrie over Ed.</li>
<li>Gat sees racism and privilege.</li>
<li>Summer fifteen = the fire, not a swimming accident.</li>
<li>Gasoline and the timing mistake made the fire spread too fast.</li>
<li>Gat, Johnny, Mirren and the dogs Fatima and Prince Philip die.</li>
<li>Cadence burns her hands and feet trying to save them.</li>
<li>Penny loves Cady "in spite of" what she may have done.</li>
<li>Motive: stop the family's greed and fighting; the plan was dangerous.</li></ol></section>

<section id="vocab"><h2>8. Vocabulary and sentence starters</h2>
<div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Word</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Deutsch</th></tr>
